                </layout>
              </layoutDesc>
            </objectDesc>
            <handDesc>
              <handNote scribe="sole">There seems little doubt that this MS. is written in the hand of the famous astrologer and alchemist <persName key="person_39386049">John Dee</persName>. On pp. 15, 39, 49, 58 and 81 the characteristic initials J.D. are found, identical with those in authentic Dee MSS. On p. 13 is the small 'ladder sign' mentioned by M. R. James in his 'Preface to the List of MSS. formerly owned by Dr. John Dee', 1921.</handNote>
              <handNote script="hybrida">Written partly in italic, and partly in a secretary hand.</handNote>
            </handDesc>
            <decoDesc>
              <decoNote type="illustration">On p. 25 is a rough pen-drawing of a 'longe receptory' used in the sublimation process.</decoNote>
            </decoDesc>
            <additions/>
            <bindingDesc>
              <binding>
                <p>Unbound; cover is printed sheet which is identified as gathering "Q" from William Perkins' 'Armilla aurea … editio secunda' (Cambridge, 1591?). STC (2nd ed.) 19655a.</p>
              </binding>
            </bindingDesc>
          </physDesc>
          <history>
            <origin>
              <origDate>Late 16th century</origDate>
            </origin>
            <provenance>Inside the first blank leaf is written in pencil 'Horwoods List No. 101'. (I.e. Alfred John Horwood [1821-1881], Inspector in the Royal Commission on Historical Manuscripts.) Inserted is a note on this MS. on four leaves, probably by the Cataloguer at Sotheby's.</provenance>
            <acquisition>Purchased at Sotheby's 17/2/1931, Lot 359A (accession 56328).</acquisition>
          </history>
